ly on other melds. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I should discard T4. Then I have T2 and T7. \n\nBut this seems not helpful. \n\nWait, maybe the problem is that I have too many single tiles. To form four melds, I need to have four groups of three or four (kongs). \n\nGiven that I have a chow of B2-B3-B4 and a pair of W1, I need three more melds. \n\nLooking at the remaining tiles: T2, T4, T7, F2, F3, F4, J3x2. \n\nIs there any way to form three melds from these? \n\nFor example, if I have T2 and T4, but no T3. F2, F3, F4 can't form a meld. J3 is two, which could be a pair. \n\nSo maybe the only way is to have two more chows and a pair. But with the current tiles, it's impossible. \n\nThis suggests that my current hand is not in a good position. Maybe I need to discard a tile that allows me to form another chow. \n\nWait, what if I discard T7, keeping T2 and T4. Then if I can get T3, I can form T2-T3-T4. \n\nBut I have to discard a tile now. \n\nAlternatively, discard B7, keeping B2-B3-B4 as a chow. \n\nBut even then, I need three more melds. \n\nMaybe I'm missing something. Let's check if there are any other possible melds. \n\nFor example, if I have F2, F3, F4. If I can get another F2, F3, or F4 to form a pung. But I only have one each. \n\nSo maybe the best is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. \n\nBetween B7 and T7, either is fine. Let's say I choose to discard B7. \n\nAfter discarding B7, I draw a new tile. Depending on what I draw, I can form more melds. \n\nFor example, if I draw a T3, I can form T2-T3-T4. If I draw a J3, I can have three J3s (but I only have two now). \n\nAlternatively, if I draw a F2, F3, or F4, I can form a pung with one of them. \n\nBut since I'm discarding B7 now, which is not part of any current meld, it's the least impactful. \n\nSo the answer would be to play B7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has a potential chow in B2-B3-B4 and a pair of W1. Discarding B7, which is a single tile not part of any meld, maximizes flexibility for future melds.\nReason: B7 is a single tile without potential for chows or pungs, while retaining B2-B3-B4 allows forming a chow. Discarding it preserves the possibility of completing other melds like T2-T3-T4 if T3 is drawn.\nAnswer: Play B7"}
