rs is worth 1 point each. Since they have a Pung of J3, that's 1 point. \\n\\nIf they can form another Pung of Honors, like F2 or F4, but they only have one each. \\n\\nBut that's not possible. \\n\\nSo the main focus is on the chows. \\n\\nThe best move is to discard a tile that's not part of the potential chows. \\n\\nBetween T2 and T4, they need T3 to form a chow. So discarding either allows them to wait for T3. \\n\\nSince both are possible, maybe it's better to discard the one that's less likely to be needed. \\n\\nLooking at the unshown tiles, T2 and T4 have the same number left. \\n\\nAlternatively, maybe discard W7, since it's not part of any chow. \\n\\nBut W7 is a single tile. \\n\\nIf they discard W7, they can wait for other tiles. \\n\\nBut the player across has a meld of W7-W8-W9, so those are already out. \\n\\nSo maybe W7 is not useful. \\n\\nTherefore, the best options are to discard T2, T4, W7, F2, or F4. \\n\\nBetween these, discarding T2 or T4 gives a chance to form a chow with T3. \\n\\nSo I think the best choice is to discard T2 or T4. \\n\\nLooking at the valid actions, the user can choose any of them. \\n\\nBut which one? \\n\\nIf they discard T2, they have T4 and T7. If they get T3, they can form T2-T3-T4. \\n\\nIf they discard T4, they have T2 and T7. If they get T3, they can form T2-T3-T4. \\n\\nEither way, they need T3. \\n\\nBut since they have T2 and T4 in hand, maybe it's better to discard one of them. \\n\\nI think the safest is to discard T4. \\n\\nSo the answer would be Play T4.\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: The hand has a potential chow in B2-B3-B4 and a pair in W1. To form additional melds, discarding T4 allows waiting for T3 to complete a T2-T3-T4 chow. Other options like T2 or W7 are less critical for potential melds.\\nReason: Discarding T4 maintains T2 for a possible chow with T3, which is still available in unshown tiles. This maximizes the chance to form a winning hand with multiple melds.\\nAnswer: Play T4\"}"}
